#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](images/ba/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568" :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](images/0f/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0959e013-21cc-54d6-8102-8411aae2cef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i+j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259960} {:answer/id #uuid "3505bb37-de2c-5217-b17b-cf55b32fcafd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260347} {:answer/id #uuid "63e13c8f-9ffe-50e8-bdf5-12f885eeb52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260738} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cb1df1-27ea-5795-b0d7-f5b973e46cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261307} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7b377c-2eff-5409-b057-56b76067128a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7b377c-2eff-5409-b057-56b76067128a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261370} :answer-field/id #uuid "9face947-83ba-5ccc-922a-35b3f8f14bde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "408e688a-67ac-5189-9731-466fc02feb87" :question/math-academy-id "q-126738" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-j-k \\\\ (1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k & =2\\,i-j-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-t=2 \\\\ -{t}^{2}=-1 \\\\ 2t+1=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(-1).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-2t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =-i-2(-1)\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is $-i+2\\,j+2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f9c607-a00d-5f26-9c10-092b5fb19c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨-2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433156} {:answer/id #uuid "35a27e71-b8e5-549e-97ab-d093597bcb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433157} {:answer/id #uuid "3e141537-57e7-5d24-b24a-a74eaabb1717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩+u⟨4,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433158} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3eb033-52d1-5b96-94b9-64ac07d6af16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433164} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a9f5e6-8ed5-5aa2-8980-b3e5d5b6e091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩+u⟨4,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3eb033-52d1-5b96-94b9-64ac07d6af16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433164} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb45a89e-3bde-5251-b24c-b1bd686d3497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8d1a00d-2251-5725-a02b-3b2319318fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126832" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+3,\\,1-t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1),$ valid for all real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+3,1-t,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,0,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the components yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t+3=4 \\\\ 1-t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore,  $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$\n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-1,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point  $P(4,0,1)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a35b6d-2cae-582e-8493-f87cca09de5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400249} {:answer/id #uuid "89946061-3179-5a62-92a4-c8e5a2b9e7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400250} {:answer/id #uuid "4d287fc9-fb22-5b2f-ba9a-16affbe14ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400251} {:answer/id #uuid "09e9c576-9966-5982-b9d6-7c94b62a04c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400252} {:answer/id #uuid "c517880b-005f-5788-8339-983446cb7ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d287fc9-fb22-5b2f-ba9a-16affbe14ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400251} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d09d6d-fdd3-564f-8577-1f47d907f393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1276b48a-33df-5447-9033-1c8e5ee23a8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126841" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52960168-62b7-525a-ac7b-2f022f67c7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260573} {:answer/id #uuid "74adb08b-0d41-527b-8729-2207aa1ec50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{11}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260897} {:answer/id #uuid "b34633b7-6742-5219-9d90-8217c43d8703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{2},\\frac{5}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261457} {:answer/id #uuid "d32f0660-7e5d-51fe-8d96-78c943404e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261732} {:answer/id #uuid "ed712fb1-45f1-55a0-a59c-6fe12a47ce3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed712fb1-45f1-55a0-a59c-6fe12a47ce3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261961} :answer-field/id #uuid "0db3121b-67b4-5c2e-8259-22376f538fb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dd4daf7-0ad9-5d7d-adaf-5ba41fb5fc8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126896" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨1-{t}^{2},3t+4⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2t,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1(-2t)+1(3) & =0 \\\\ -2t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨1-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},3(\\frac{3}{2})+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{2}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bc61bf1-f972-5eab-b918-e8f26ae77f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260114} {:answer/id #uuid "35651e20-38c6-5134-9b41-f71d40d68e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260352} {:answer/id #uuid "4102c4c5-f376-5ad2-973b-cd16b3dcbd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260444} {:answer/id #uuid "ce664148-6568-55f7-b810-5c395b9e2281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261700} {:answer/id #uuid "da332111-1057-5984-b86a-48b0a295abe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce664148-6568-55f7-b810-5c395b9e2281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261700} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5934d8-dbdd-52b9-ac7b-f848ea28f57e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77999ddb-4941-5816-87bf-4391b434e5de" :question/math-academy-id "q-126898" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t+2,-2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0(2t+2)+(-1)(-2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{2}+2(0),4-{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f7dbd3-9ac6-59ba-822d-baee2e1a48d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad7606a-78cf-5b5a-aad3-516d0783136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410819} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2a15f9-1731-51c6-a7aa-e028d3d2c84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410820} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e66482-ad5e-56f0-ab9c-0187ad900545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410821} {:answer/id #uuid "c15d36f2-d954-5b67-a997-5ab2ddb7afaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f7dbd3-9ac6-59ba-822d-baee2e1a48d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410818} :answer-field/id #uuid "236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-126900" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t,4t-1⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33803699-b04f-5482-93b1-eecd1d726df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-4)$ and $(-4,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282150} {:answer/id #uuid "88d8fb0d-e1b8-554b-8326-975ff398328a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,8)$ and $(9,12)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282870} {:answer/id #uuid "b1770175-f1e5-5bc7-b4c5-be7f98218790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283224} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cedca4-c879-5bdf-8186-06540935689b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283229} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dc1b6b-a24c-550f-aac1-4366f72f5fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-8,0)$ and $(-7,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1770175-f1e5-5bc7-b4c5-be7f98218790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283224} :answer-field/id #uuid "a241fdc7-dfa3-5484-a79a-a0cfc2eb80e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e59e7b4c-f2b2-52bd-8897-e35a51c56df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-164836" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-8)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i-4\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-8)\\cdot 2t+(-4t)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3}-16t+16t & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=({0}^{2}-8)\\,i-4(0)\\,j=-8i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-8,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e651f70-c5ab-567f-a036-5f7d5fda5c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281963} {:answer/id #uuid "44e6520f-63d5-561c-a5db-3cf7509b2166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{5})$ and $(1,\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282273} {:answer/id #uuid "91d3ac21-ee56-5c77-adf0-9225f010ea01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282950} {:answer/id #uuid "a797455e-01a6-53b3-844b-b3b66ee0a8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283146} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c70d08-002d-5332-bd5a-e3accf2c0b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a797455e-01a6-53b3-844b-b3b66ee0a8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283146} :answer-field/id #uuid "821576ac-c30f-5e83-a325-353b73ea58d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94fb91b9-85ca-5af1-8e63-64ced0f72975" :question/math-academy-id "q-164838" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j]\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (t+2)\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}} & =0 \\\\ t+2+t & =0 \\\\ 2t+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1)=(-1+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d65ba429-12ce-5d3f-aead-951c7c493fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i + 2j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117434} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e12f8d-a616-5746-8254-4a76b7946403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i + j + 3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117435} {:answer/id #uuid "1a080b7a-c87c-58ae-8fbe-02d5b6a609bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i + 2j + 3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117436} {:answer/id #uuid "408ac6bf-0261-5ded-a895-4424eb4c8e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i + j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117437} {:answer/id #uuid "559050b6-1067-540d-aede-86e48b660538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2j + 3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117438}] :answer-field/id #uuid "157a8eff-7632-56b1-9580-5dd330307dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117433}] :question/id #uuid "3fb55fdf-0c58-5431-9646-b68e9db8c5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-32872" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = 2ti + (1 + t^{2})j + t^{3}k$ at the point $P(2, 2, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9a33aa7-a1f9-5b05-b87e-72419fcb6afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 1⟩ + u⟨ - 1, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117452} {:answer/id #uuid "b1712e94-8595-5e1d-8b26-ae6897a84ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 1⟩ + u⟨ - 1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117453} {:answer/id #uuid "cd005fbb-9570-5647-b3dd-5ea28ac483ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 1⟩ + u⟨1, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117454} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8dd508-5219-56b0-bd9c-dd1941f1db0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 1⟩ + u⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117455} {:answer/id #uuid "13397b8a-c3d9-54e3-98d3-8f331899b410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 1⟩ + u⟨1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117456}] :answer-field/id #uuid "304fbebf-027a-547b-982b-e2a3aeecd5e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117451}] :question/id #uuid "3b163add-1f12-505d-95b5-8027481def13" :question/math-academy-id "q-32917" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t} - 1, e^{-2t}⟩$ at the point $P(0, 1)$, valid for all real values of $u$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d14831-aea7-5347-84f9-25f41978fb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305224} {:answer/id #uuid "637c5e5e-6ce7-5938-b14c-a011022a3175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306115} {:answer/id #uuid "7f64699b-83aa-58d4-93aa-cd90febe5c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306371} {:answer/id #uuid "80bcb621-3c43-5efd-84e5-b398b7670b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306377} {:answer/id #uuid "cffb659a-2557-57d8-a5c5-5fc2ab177da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+2\\sqrt{5}\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f64699b-83aa-58d4-93aa-cd90febe5c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306371} :answer-field/id #uuid "e25a7c47-96dc-50c7-b0ca-55f528b99c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eafab8fd-4a82-53c3-b03e-eaa84e01b098" :question/math-academy-id "q-33457" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j+(t-1)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=2,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=2$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(2)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(2)+u{r}^{′}(2),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $r(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =({2}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}}\\,j+(2-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =2(2)\\,i+\\frac{(2)}{\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=2$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154048e9-acbe-5f59-9e18-820fa18ba04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305335} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaff3a3-015e-5ac9-af35-ee4636f31af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306017} {:answer/id #uuid "946e8ca8-3d7d-5f49-966f-fe22ae7ccf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306552} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d8a392-1288-5247-80cb-5a23331deecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb277c8e-5a50-5fc4-b7fe-bc09685bb676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb277c8e-5a50-5fc4-b7fe-bc09685bb676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307478} :answer-field/id #uuid "9129fe60-9337-5fc0-b0ac-28b086b1002c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df456207-ebf6-517a-a452-6527239e70c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-33461" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c02fa6d-7f44-5009-aeb5-73947ff82f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342345} {:answer/id #uuid "13a67418-5132-576d-9b5f-54e888569d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342411} {:answer/id #uuid "4c36ed04-abb6-5227-afda-fdb9d47a92bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342867} {:answer/id #uuid "6c61a597-2ad7-5cb4-aa85-2d830d18a9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343164} {:answer/id #uuid "a3095862-d2d5-5438-9738-e4a71223c354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a67418-5132-576d-9b5f-54e888569d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342411} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd730d9-0504-5efa-a7c0-b5d6d589a3c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "607c69e9-9d6e-5de2-af4e-cf5bd02dd46c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75197" :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t-\\frac{1}{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=1$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(1)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}},\\,1+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-1,\\,1+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1$  is  $⟨0,\\,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071926e4-2595-5703-95c2-3cacd4f8a62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342300} {:answer/id #uuid "1658bdd9-2525-5b12-b11d-9819d1d32658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342436} {:answer/id #uuid "4112ead0-2136-5a12-97ad-269c476f708d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342774} {:answer/id #uuid "525c7271-6d02-5369-9923-9a0e4a040017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342922} {:answer/id #uuid "9aaf565d-b754-562f-b95b-3208ecbf1b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4112ead0-2136-5a12-97ad-269c476f708d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342774} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec141fef-f356-543a-9493-dcd07decf59a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d979bdae-e51f-5a47-9ba4-fdf00565f442" :question/math-academy-id "q-75199" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0},\\,2\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is $⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.$"}]}
